## Reflection

<details style="margin-top: 12px; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;">
  <summary style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 12px 16px; cursor: pointer; font-weight: bold; color: #1F1976; font-size: 16px;">
    &#128161; How does probability connect back to what machine learning actually is?
  </summary>
  <div style="background-color: #FFFFFF; padding: 16px; border: 1px solid #ACA8EA; font-size: 16px; color: #1A1A1A; line-height: 1.6;">
    Lesson 2 states this almost directly: "AI doesn't always know the answer for sure, so it needs to make a prediction... we need strategies that let AI measure how confident it is about events that are uncertain." A machine learning model isn't claiming certainty when it predicts "dog," it's expressing a probability. Understanding what probability actually measures is what lets students later interpret a model's confidence meaningfully, rather than treating every prediction as a flat yes or no.
  </div>
</details>

